CS 214 Spring 2026 - Project IV: A Simple Chat Server
Authors: Haaris Toor (hbt20), Hassan Ibrahim (hi125)

OVERVIEW
========
chatd is a multithreaded TCP chat server that supports multiple simultaneous
clients. Clients authenticate with a unique screen name, send public or private
messages, set a status string, and query connected users.

BUILDING
========
    make          # builds chatd and test_client
    make clean    # removes binaries and object files

Requires: gcc, pthreads. Tested on Linux (ILAB) and macOS.

USAGE
=====
    ./chatd <port>

Example:
    ./chatd 9999

PROTOCOL SUMMARY
================
All messages follow the format: VERSION|CODE|LENGTH|BODY
- VERSION: always "1"
- CODE: NAM, SET, MSG, WHO, or ERR
- LENGTH: byte count of BODY (including the trailing '|')
- BODY: pipe-delimited fields ending with '|'

Message types:
  NAM  Client sets screen name:   body = screen_name|
  SET  Client sets status:        body = status|
  MSG  Client sends message:      body = sender|recipient|text|
                                  (sender field is empty/ignored from client)
  WHO  Client queries user info:  body = name| or #all|
  ERR  Server error response:     body = code|explanation|

Error codes:
  0 - Unreadable message (fatal: connection closed)
  1 - Screen name in use
  2 - Unknown recipient
  3 - Illegal character in field
  4 - Field too long

IMPLEMENTATION NOTES
====================
- One thread per client; clients_mutex protects the global client list.
- Per-client accumulation buffer handles partial TCP reads and multiple
  messages arriving in a single recv() call.
- MSG body parsed with strchr/strrchr (not strtok) to correctly handle
  the empty sender field and '|' characters inside message text.
- Duplicate-name check and client-list insertion are atomic under the mutex.
- SIGPIPE is ignored; failed sends to dead clients return EPIPE silently.
- Sender field in MSG is always overwritten with client->screen_name
  (prevents spoofing).

Field limits:
  Screen names: 1-32 characters (letters, digits, '-', '_')
  Status:       0-64 characters (ASCII 32-126)
  Message text: 1-80 characters (ASCII 32-126)

TEST PLAN
=========
Automated tests are in test.sh. Run with:
    bash test.sh

The script starts chatd on port 9999, sends raw protocol messages via nc
(netcat), captures responses, and checks for expected content.

Test 1: NAM — successful authentication
  Send:   1|NAM|6|Alice|
  Expect: response contains "Welcome to the chat"
  Checks: server sends MSG welcome on successful NAM

Test 2: Duplicate screen name (ERR 1)
  Setup:  Connect Alice and keep connection open (sleep 2)
  Send:   1|NAM|6|Alice| from a second connection
  Expect: response contains "already in use"
  Checks: atomic duplicate detection; second client rejected with ERR 1

Test 3: Invalid screen name characters (ERR 3)
  Send:   1|NAM|6|Bob@!|
  Expect: response contains "ERR"
  Checks: validate_screen_name rejects '@' and '!'

Test 4: SET status — multi-message session
  Send:   1|NAM|4|Bob|1|SET|10|Chatting!|  (two messages, no separator)
  Expect: response contains "Bob is now"
  Checks: accumulation buffer correctly processes second message after NAM;
          SET handler broadcasts status change to #all

Test 5: WHO single user
  Setup:  Connect Carol with status "Online!" (sleep 10 to keep alive)
  Send:   1|NAM|5|Dave|1|WHO|6|Carol|
  Expect: response contains "Carol: Online!"
  Checks: WHO returns name+status for named user; status survives across
          messages in the same buffered session

Test 6: WHO #all
  Send:   1|NAM|4|Eve|1|WHO|5|#all|
  Expect: response contains "Eve"
  Checks: WHO #all lists all currently authenticated clients

Test 7: MSG broadcast to #all
  Send:   1|NAM|6|Frank|1|MSG|22||#all|Hello everyone!|
  Expect: response contains "Frank|#all|Hello everyone"
  Checks: empty sender field parsed correctly; broadcast MSG echoed back
          to sender; sender field overwritten with screen_name

Test 8: MSG private message (no error)
  Setup:  Connect Grace (sleep 10 to keep alive)
  Send:   1|NAM|5|Hank|1|MSG|23||Grace|Private message|
  Expect: no ERR response (exit 0 from nc)
  Checks: private message delivered without error when recipient exists

Test 9: MSG to unknown recipient (ERR 2)
  Send:   1|NAM|4|Ivy|1|MSG|19||NoSuchUser|Hello?|
  Expect: response contains "User not found"
  Checks: handle_msg_message returns ERR 2 for non-existent recipient

Test 10: Invalid protocol version (ERR 0, connection closed)
  Send:   0|NAM|4|Bob|
  Expect: response matches pattern ERR|<N>|0|
  Checks: parse_message rejects version != "1"; server sends ERR 0 and
          closes the connection (fatal error path)

Manual testing was also performed using ./test_client to exercise
interactive sessions: connecting multiple clients, sending messages between
them, and verifying delivery in real time.
